I found two gaps in the written argument: the Möbius sum must retain \(d\le Q'\) when Drappeau is applied, and \(Q'<y^K\) needs a separate sentence because the large-conductor contribution is empty. Both have direct repairs. The transfer cost is polynomial in \(\log x\), and the saving absorbs it. The choice of \(K\) leaves a margin of \(y^{3/2-o(1)}\) in the large-sieve estimate.

