1. **OK — §2(D), Drappeau’s statement and §5(a) application.** The quotation is correct: \(Q\le x\), conductor \(1<\operatorname{cond}\chi\le x^{\eta_D}\), all characters including imprimitive ones, arbitrary fixed \(A>0\), and effectivity for \(A<1\). Restricting its nonnegative left side to primitive pairs \(q=r\le R_1\) is legitimate. With \(d\le Q'\), \(z=x/d\ge x^{1/2+\lambda}\), so uniformly
   \[
   R_1=y^K=z^{o(1)}\le z^{\eta_D},\qquad (\log z)^{c_D}\le y\le z.
   \]
   No contradiction with Harper’s (3.1) emerges. [Drappeau, Lemme 5](https://arxiv.org/html/1307.7554v3).

2. **MINOR — §5(a), the displayed Möbius sum drops an essential cutoff.** After replacing \(W(r,d)\), the note writes an unrestricted \(\sum_d D(x/d)\), then asserts \(d\le Q'\). That restriction has disappeared from the displayed expression. For unrestricted squarefree smooth \(d\), \(x/d\) can be below \(y\), invalidating Drappeau’s hypotheses and \(u_d\ge u/2\).

   **Fix:** retain \(d\le Q'\) through the application of Drappeau and dBT. Extend the resulting positive Euler sum to all squarefree \(d\) only afterward. Originally \(rd\le Q'\), so this repair costs nothing.

3. **MINOR — §5(b), illegal application when \(Q'<R_1\).** Lemma SM allows \(2y\le Q'<y^K\), whereas LS′ requires \(R_0\le Q_0\). The advertised substitution \(R_0=R_1,\ Q_0=Q'\) then violates its hypotheses.

   **Fix:** state that case (b) is empty when \(Q'\le R_1\), since every conductor divides \(s\le Q'\). Apply LS′ only when \(Q'>R_1\). The main application has \(Q'\ge x^\lambda/2\gg R_1\), so this affects the broader SM statement only.

4. **OK — E3, E8 and §5(a), transfer weights.** The bookkeeping works:
   \[
   W(r,d)\ll \log\log Q'\,\varrho(Q',y)
   \frac{r^{1-\beta'}d^{1-\beta'}}{\varphi(r)\varphi(d)}.
   \]
   Coprimality \((r,d)=1\) supplies \(\varphi(rd)=\varphi(r)\varphi(d)\); overlapping factors in \(j\) cause no reverse inequality. After the local bound, the remaining divisor sum is
   \[
   \sum_{d\ \mathrm{squarefree}}\frac{d^{1-\beta-\beta'}}{\varphi(d)}
   \le\prod_p\left(1+\frac{p^{-1/2}}{p-1}\right)<6.
   \]
   There is no hidden \(2^{\omega(s)}\) loss. Even for moduli with many prime factors, the pointwise totient loss is only \(O(\log\log Q')\).

5. **OK — §5(a), absorption of the transfer cost.** Put
   \[
   V_N=\frac{\sqrt{\log N}}{(\log\log N)^{5/2}}.
   \]
   The calculation gives
   \[
   \delta_D\log H(u/2)=\left(\frac{2\delta_D\xi}{a}+o(1)\right)V_N.
   \]
   Its coefficient is at least \(4c_0+o(1)\). Meanwhile
   \[
   \log\!\left[\log\log x\,(\log x)^{K(1+C_\alpha)}\right]
   =O(\log\log N)=o(V_N).
   \]
   Thus the claimed \(\varepsilon_N\) survives with substantial slack. Fixed, possibly large \(K\) does not break this argument.

6. **OK — E4 and §4, factorisation, Perron and bilinear estimate.** E4 is unique, including repeated equal primes. The coefficient sequences are character independent and supported within intervals of lengths at most \(y\sqrt z\) and \(\sqrt z\). Perron separates the remaining condition \(mk\le z\).

   The half-integer \(z'\) gives \(|z'-mk|\ge1/2\); the claimed \(|\log(z'/mk)|\ge1/(4yz)\) is valid. With \(T=(2zR)^4\), the aggregate truncation error is at most \(ey^3/(z^2R^2)\le e\). The integral and Cauchy–Schwarz estimates have enough slack for constant \(30\). In LS′, the final coefficients are at most \(480\) and \(1440\), both below \(1500\).

7. **OK — E7, §5(b), §6 Part 2: the large-sieve saving is sufficient.** The distinct-prime-products argument proves the crude density bound. Its exponent is correctly
   \[
   \frac{u\log(4u\log y)}{\log y}=\frac{\xi}{a^2}+o(1).
   \]
   Writing \(b=\xi_{\max}/a^2\), the first LS′ term divided by \(\Psi(x,y)\varrho(Q',y)\) is
   \[
   y^{9/2-K+2b+o(1)}\le y^{-3/2+o(1)}.
   \]
   The second is \(x^{-\lambda}y^{O(1)}\). The additional \(\log Y\le\log x\) in Part 2 is \(y^{o(1)}\). Both errors are smaller than \(\varepsilon_N\). Conductors immediately above \(y^K\) are covered.

8. **OK — E2 and the residue maximum.** The decomposition is exact. It requires \(\ell>y\) and \((\ell,s)=1\), but does **not** require every prime factor of \(s\) to be at most \(y\). Smoothness supplies the coprimality automatically here. Every reduced class modulo \(s\) has \(\ell-1\) reduced lifts modulo \(\ell s\), so replacing the maximum over reduced \(r\bmod t\) by the maximum modulo \(s\) is valid.

9. **OK — §6(i), counting.** HT Theorem 3 is used only with fixed multipliers \(2\) and \(4\), within its range. Uniformly, \(Q/(4Y)\ge x^\lambda/4\), hence \(u_z\to\infty\) and \(\alpha(z,y)\to1\). The stated constants give
   \[
   0.19\cdot\frac35=0.114>0.1.
   \]
   Thus the claimed lower constant \(1/10\) follows; the upper constant \(3\) also has slack.

10. **OK — §1 interface and FM Step 3.** The final design supplies
    \[
    \mu-\nu\ge\eta_0,\qquad
    \xi/2-(\mu+\nu)\ge\eta_0/2.
    \]
    Choosing \(\lambda=\eta_0/(4\xi_{\max})\) leaves fixed positive margins. Every grid point has exponent \(\xi+o(1)\), since its displacement is \(L^{O(1)}\), so one fixed compact \(\Xi\) works uniformly. Also
    \[
    (\log\omega)\varepsilon_N\log\log N
    =\exp\{-c_0V_N+O(\log\log N)\}\longrightarrow0.
    \]
    The replacement saving is sufficient for FM’s bad-modulus argument.

